          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"lafayette-la-princesse-de-cleves-4edd91c3c0ffd342\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.98,\"analysis\":\"The earlier spoken and written declarations represented by « je vous aimais » present the proposition that the woman loved her addressee as true, so their core force is strongly truth-conditional even though the declarations were calculated and misleading. They neither undertake a relational commitment nor function as affect-driven verbal discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The phrase collectively reports both spoken and written declarations, and its imperfect tense occurs under past-tense indirect discourse; neither complication materially changes the core T classification.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"reported\",\"description\":\"Within a letter, the writer retrospectively reports earlier occasions on which she told and wrote to the addressee that she loved him.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The declarations were deliberate acts of dissimulation. After learning of the addressee's infidelity, the writer sought to conceal her changed feelings, rekindle his attachment, and punish his pride. Her difficulty in producing reassuring declarations inadvertently revealed that she no longer loved him. This strategic deception affects sincerity and uptake but not the declarations' truth-presenting core force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"« je me faisais une si grande violence pour vous dire et pour vous écrire que je vous aimais »\",\"supports\":\"Identifies reported spoken and written avowals whose semantic content presents the writer's love as true; the effort involved also weighs against reflexive E force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"« Je crus que si quelque chose pouvait rallumer les sentiments que vous aviez eus pour moi, c'était de vous faire voir que les miens étaient changés; mais de vous le faire voir en feignant de vous le cacher »\",\"supports\":\"Shows that the prior declarations were calculated components of a strategy rather than spontaneous expressive discharges.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"« Je tâchais de vous rassurer; mais c'était d'une manière si forcée, que vous en étiez encore mieux persuadé que je ne vous aimais plus. »\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the avowals attempted reassurance while presenting love as continuing, but were forced and unconvincing; this supports high T and E=0 without implying P.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"« je vous ai fait voir que je ne vous aimais plus »\",\"supports\":\"Retrospectively establishes the discrepancy between the love asserted in the earlier declarations and the writer's actual changed feelings, making deception contextual rather than an ontology failure.\",\"confidence\":0.98}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the strategically asserted loving state. Calculation, insincerity, reassurance, and retaliatory manipulation are contextual features and create no residual core force requiring P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The supplied analytical translation was not needed for the judgement; the French local context provides direct evidence.\"}"
        }
